1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,994
Orchard Core manages the content, and Blazor draws the pages.

2
00:00:04,994 --> 00:00:08,928
In this video, we build a Blazor Server front end in a Razor

3
00:00:08,928 --> 00:00:13,935
class library, serve it from Orchard Core, render content items, add a tenant,

4
00:00:13,935 --> 00:00:16,260
and make a component interactive.

5
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:25,258
Editors write and publish content in the Orchard Core admin.

6
00:00:25,258 --> 00:00:28,924
A Blazor front end turns that content into pages.

7
00:00:28,924 --> 00:00:34,921
Both run in one ASP.NET Core application, so components read content through Orchard

8
00:00:34,921 --> 00:00:37,920
Core services, not through an HTTP API.

9
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:42,107
The Blazor app lives in a Razor class library.

10
00:00:42,107 --> 00:00:48,916
The web project references the library, and Program.cs maps it into the pipeline of every tenant.

11
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:54,270
The library holds the App component, the router, the layout, and the pages.

12
00:00:58,570 --> 00:01:01,705
The site is set up with the Headless site recipe.

13
00:01:01,705 --> 00:01:04,953
A recipe is a list of steps that Orchard Core runs.

14
00:01:04,953 --> 00:01:10,025
Under Tools, Deployments, JSON Import, you paste a recipe that creates the Markdown

15
00:01:10,025 --> 00:01:13,160
Page content type and an item, and select Import.

16
00:01:13,632 --> 00:01:20,332
Under Content, Content Items, the item is listed with its title and its type, MarkdownPage.

17
00:01:20,332 --> 00:01:25,582
A Markdown Page has a title, an alias, and a body written in Markdown.

18
00:01:26,043 --> 00:01:27,633
Let's open the item.

19
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:29,590
Its alias is orchard-core.

20
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:33,994
The Blazor front end finds the item by this alias, and renders its

21
00:01:33,994 --> 00:01:35,523
title and its body.

22
00:01:39,723 --> 00:01:44,593
Blazor components go in a Razor class library, because Razor components are not

23
00:01:44,593 --> 00:01:46,885
supported in Orchard Core modules.

24
00:01:46,885 --> 00:01:51,469
Create the library, reference it from the web project, and add a framework

25
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:53,303
reference to ASP.NET Core.

26
00:01:54,053 --> 00:01:56,189
App.razor is the root component.

27
00:01:56,189 --> 00:02:01,023
It links the Bootstrap style sheet from the Orchard Core Resources module, loads

28
00:02:01,023 --> 00:02:04,284
the Blazor script, and renders the Routes component.

29
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:08,163
Routes holds the router, with MainLayout as the default layout.

30
00:02:08,913 --> 00:02:14,295
MainLayout shows the navigation menu in a sidebar, and each page in the content area.

31
00:02:14,295 --> 00:02:16,070
NavMenu holds the links.

32
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:20,742
The Home page listens on slash home, because Orchard Core also listens on

33
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:24,173
the root path, for example to show the Setup screen.

34
00:02:28,373 --> 00:02:34,510
In Program.cs, the project template leaves a commented block for the Orchard Specific Pipeline.

35
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:38,217
Fill it with two callbacks of the Orchard Core builder.

36
00:02:38,217 --> 00:02:43,443
ConfigureServices registers Razor components with interactive server components.

37
00:02:44,193 --> 00:02:49,431
Configure adds static files and anti-forgery, and maps the App component with the

38
00:02:49,431 --> 00:02:51,658
interactive server render mode.

39
00:02:51,658 --> 00:02:56,475
Orchard Core runs both callbacks for each tenant, so every tenant gets its

40
00:02:56,475 --> 00:02:58,883
own Blazor services and endpoints.

41
00:03:03,083 --> 00:03:06,182
The Content page takes an alias from its route.

42
00:03:06,182 --> 00:03:14,718
It injects IContentHandleManager, IContentManager, and ISiteService, which come with one package reference to OrchardCore.ContentManagement.

43
00:03:14,718 --> 00:03:18,343
The site service gives the site name for the page title.

44
00:03:19,093 --> 00:03:24,679
The handle manager turns the alias prefix and the alias into a content item ID.

45
00:03:24,679 --> 00:03:30,593
The content manager loads the published version, and the Body aspect gives its HTML.

46
00:03:30,593 --> 00:03:35,193
An unknown alias gives no item, and the aspect call then throws.

47
00:03:35,943 --> 00:03:40,961
The markup shows Loading until the item exists, then the title and the body.

48
00:03:40,961 --> 00:03:45,917
MarkupString writes the HTML as it is, and the Markdown body part sanitizes

49
00:03:45,917 --> 00:03:49,283
it by default, through its Sanitize Html setting.

50
00:03:50,033 --> 00:03:55,555
StreamRendering sends the page right away, with Loading, and streams the content in

51
00:03:55,555 --> 00:03:57,354
when the work finishes.

52
00:03:57,354 --> 00:04:03,063
The guide adds a half-second delay in OnInitializedAsync to mimic a long-running task.

53
00:04:04,763 --> 00:04:05,963
Let's run the site.

54
00:04:05,963 --> 00:04:09,515
The sidebar and the Home page come from the Razor class library, and

55
00:04:09,515 --> 00:04:11,003
Orchard Core serves them.

56
00:04:11,505 --> 00:04:14,214
Select Orchard Core in the sidebar.

57
00:04:14,214 --> 00:04:18,640
The page loads the item by its alias, and shows its title and

58
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:21,085
its Markdown, rendered as HTML.

59
00:04:25,385 --> 00:04:30,457
A tenant is a separate site with its own content, served by the same application.

60
00:04:30,457 --> 00:04:35,238
Tenants are managed from the default tenant, where the feature is available.

61
00:04:35,238 --> 00:04:40,485
Under Tools, Features, search for Tenants, and select Enable on the Tenants feature.

62
00:04:40,943 --> 00:04:44,658
Under Multi-Tenancy, Tenants, select Add Tenant.

63
00:04:44,658 --> 00:04:49,267
Enter FirstTenant as the Name and tenant01 as the Url Prefix.

64
00:04:49,267 --> 00:04:54,083
Choose Headless site as the Recipe, so the tenant is set up like

65
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:56,973
the default site, and select Create.

66
00:04:58,953 --> 00:05:03,417
The tenant is listed as Uninitialized, with a Setup button.

67
00:05:03,417 --> 00:05:08,363
Setup opens the Setup screen of the tenant, at its own URL prefix.

68
00:05:09,963 --> 00:05:13,401
On the Setup screen, the recipe is already chosen:

69
00:05:13,401 --> 00:05:18,068
enter a site name and the super user details, and select Finish Setup.

70
00:05:18,068 --> 00:05:21,998
Then sign in to the tenant, and use JSON Import to add the

71
00:05:21,998 --> 00:05:25,253
Markdown Page type and an item for this tenant.

72
00:05:29,453 --> 00:05:32,757
A tenant with a URL prefix lives under a subpath.

73
00:05:32,757 --> 00:05:36,783
Orchard Core adds the prefix to the PathBase of each request.

74
00:05:36,783 --> 00:05:41,470
With the default base tag, relative links resolve from the root, so they

75
00:05:41,470 --> 00:05:43,513
point at the default tenant.

76
00:05:44,263 --> 00:05:51,594
So App.razor injects NavigationManager, and sets the base tag to its BaseUri in OnParametersSet.

77
00:05:51,594 --> 00:05:57,633
For a tenant, the base URI includes the URL prefix, and every link follows it.

78
00:05:58,383 --> 00:06:00,734
Other sources give the prefix too:

79
00:06:00,734 --> 00:06:06,553
the PathBase of the HttpContext request, the RequestUrlPrefix of the tenant's shell settings,

80
00:06:06,553 --> 00:06:11,255
through ShellScope, and the Base url site setting, read with ISiteService.

81
00:06:11,255 --> 00:06:14,723
That setting is empty unless you set it in the admin.

82
00:06:16,423 --> 00:06:18,733
Browse to the tenant at tenant01.

83
00:06:18,733 --> 00:06:24,123
Its pages come from the same library, and the sidebar links keep the tenant's prefix.

84
00:06:24,596 --> 00:06:26,067
Select Orchard Core.

85
00:06:26,067 --> 00:06:30,196
The page shows the item of this tenant, with its own title, because

86
00:06:30,196 --> 00:06:32,516
each tenant stores its own content.

87
00:06:36,716 --> 00:06:39,072
The pages are rendered on the server.

88
00:06:39,072 --> 00:06:42,635
To react to a click, a component declares an event handler.

89
00:06:42,635 --> 00:06:48,116
This InteractiveButton shows a button, and a Clicked method that stores the time of the click.

90
00:06:48,866 --> 00:06:54,816
The page sets the InteractiveServer render mode on the component, which makes it interactive.

91
00:06:54,816 --> 00:07:00,886
That mode relies on the interactive server services and render mode that Program.cs registered.

92
00:07:02,586 --> 00:07:04,741
Let's open the page and select Test.

93
00:07:04,741 --> 00:07:09,256
The text shows the time of the click, so the component reacted through the server.

94
00:07:10,856 --> 00:07:15,417
To see the connection, open the network tab of the browser's developer tools,

95
00:07:15,417 --> 00:07:18,055
filter on WebSocket, and refresh the page.

96
00:07:18,055 --> 00:07:22,616
The connection to the server appears, and each click sends a message over it.

97
00:07:24,616 --> 00:07:27,862
That is a Blazor Server front end for Orchard Core:

98
00:07:27,862 --> 00:07:32,988
components in a Razor class library, content read through Orchard Core services, and

99
00:07:32,988 --> 00:07:35,665
one application that serves every tenant.

100
00:07:35,665 --> 00:07:37,146
Thanks for watching.

